/* Original teaching expansions. Cases are simplified learning exercises, not diagnostic or treatment instructions. */
const GUIDE_RAW = `
foundation~Start with a normal body function, then ask what changes when that function is disturbed. Use plain words first and introduce one medical term at a time.~Cell:A basic living unit|Tissue:Cells and surrounding material organized for a shared job|Organ:A structure made from several tissues|Mechanism:The sequence that explains how something happens~Symptom/What a person experiences/Sign/What can be observed or measured~Draw one normal pathway and tell its story without medical jargon.
anatomy~Learn location, neighbors, blood supply, nerve supply and function together. These are five views of the same structure, not five unrelated lists. A lesion becomes easier to understand when you trace the structures it can affect.~Proximal:Nearer the origin or attachment of a structure|Distal:Farther from its origin or attachment|Lumen:The inner space of a tube or hollow organ|Fascia:Connective tissue that surrounds or separates structures~Artery/Carries blood away from the heart/Vein/Returns blood toward the heart~Choose a limb nerve. Name its route, one muscle action and one sensory area.
physiology~Begin with the variable a system regulates. Identify its input, sensor, response and feedback. Then change one variable mentally and predict what follows. Always keep units with equations: they often reveal whether an answer makes sense.~Perfusion:Blood flow through a tissue|Gradient:A difference that can drive movement|Compliance:Change in volume for a change in pressure|Homeostasis:Dynamic regulation of the internal environment~Ventilation/Movement of air/Perfusion/Movement of blood through tissue~Explain why a normal oxygen saturation alone does not prove normal oxygen delivery.
biochemistry~Organize each pathway by its location, purpose, starting material, products and major controls. Compare the fed state with fasting. In inherited disorders, reason through what accumulates before a block and what becomes deficient after it.~Substrate:A molecule acted on by an enzyme|Cofactor:A nonprotein helper required by some enzymes|Anabolism:Building larger molecules|Catabolism:Breaking molecules down~Transcription/DNA information used to make RNA/Translation/RNA information used to make protein~Pick a pathway and explain where it occurs, why it is needed and when it becomes active.
pathology~Use a three-part story: cause, tissue response and functional consequence. Distinguish the microscopic finding from the clinical syndrome. A pattern can suggest a mechanism without uniquely identifying its cause.~Etiology:The cause of a disease|Pathogenesis:How a disease develops|Necrosis:Cell death associated with tissue injury|Dysplasia:Disordered cellular growth and maturation~Grade/Cellular appearance and differentiation/Stage/Extent and spread of a tumor~Explain how one cellular change can produce an organ-level symptom.
pharmacology~For each class, connect target, physiological effect, useful action and predictable adverse effects. Then consider how the body handles the drug. Memorizing a brand name without a mechanism gives you little help with a new clinical context.~Agonist:Activates a receptor|Antagonist:Blocks receptor activation without activating it in the basic model|Clearance:Volume of plasma effectively cleared of drug per unit time|Bioavailability:Fraction of a dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged~Potency/Amount needed for a specified effect/Efficacy/Maximum effect achievable in the setting studied~Explain one drug class using target, effect and one mechanism-related adverse effect.
microbiology~Build an organism profile: structure, reservoir, transmission, disease pattern and diagnosis. Match the specimen to the suspected site. Think about timing, because the best test during early infection may differ from the best test later.~Antigen:A structure recognized by the immune system|Antibody:A protein made by B-cell descendants that binds an antigen|Virulence:Capacity to cause damage or disease|Colonization:Presence and growth without established disease~Sterilization/Eliminates all microbial life including spores/Disinfection/Reduces or eliminates many pathogens, depending on the method~Explain why finding an organism does not always prove it caused the symptoms.
forensic~Separate what was observed, how it was documented and what can reasonably be inferred. Preserve evidence and state uncertainty. Exact legal sections, reporting duties and time limits must be learned from current Indian law and official guidance.~Consent:An informed and voluntary decision by a person with capacity|Chain of custody:Documented handling history of evidence|Contusion:Bleeding into tissue from blunt injury|Toxidrome:A recognizable cluster of toxic effects~Observation/A finding directly recorded/Inference/An interpretation drawn from findings~Rewrite a conclusion as an observation followed by a qualified interpretation.
community~Always identify the population, numerator, denominator and time period. Decide whether you are measuring new disease, existing disease or a test result. Study public-health actions at individual, community and system levels.~Incidence:New cases in a defined population over time|Prevalence:Existing cases in a population at a specified time or period|Confounding:Distortion from a third factor linked to exposure and outcome|Bias:Systematic error in a study or measurement~Sensitivity/Positive among people with disease/Specificity/Negative among people without disease~Create a two-by-two test table and calculate one diagnostic-test measure.
medicine~Use a problem representation: age and context, time course, main symptoms, key signs and important investigation findings. Ask which mechanism best connects them. A differential should be prioritized by likelihood and urgency, not simply length.~Syndrome:A recognizable group of findings with potentially different causes|Ischemia:Inadequate blood supply relative to tissue needs|Edema:Excess fluid in interstitial tissues|Differential:Alternative explanations considered during assessment~Hypoxemia/Low oxygen in arterial blood/Hypoxia/Inadequate oxygen at tissue level~Explain one symptom through at least two different mechanisms.
surgery~Connect the anatomical problem with its physiological consequences. Ask whether the main issue is obstruction, infection, ischemia, bleeding or abnormal growth. Follow the patient from assessment through recovery; an operation is only one part of care.~Hernia:Protrusion through an opening or weakness|Anastomosis:A connection between two tubular structures|Peritonitis:Inflammation of the peritoneum|Debridement:Removal of nonviable or contaminated tissue when clinically indicated~Incarcerated hernia/Irreducible protrusion/Strangulated hernia/Compromised blood supply~Explain why an obstructed hollow organ may become an urgent problem.
obgyn~Keep ovarian events, uterine events and hormonal signals on the same timeline. During pregnancy, separate normal adaptation from pathology and always include gestational or postpartum timing in your reasoning.~Ovulation:Release of an oocyte from an ovarian follicle|Endometrium:The uterine lining|Puerperium:The period of maternal recovery after childbirth|Parity:A description of prior pregnancies reaching a defined gestational threshold~Screening/Estimates risk in a target population/Diagnosis/Establishes or excludes a condition through appropriate assessment~Draw a cycle with follicular development, ovulation and luteal function.
pediatrics~Age changes normal values, likely diagnoses, communication and assessment. Read growth as a trajectory rather than a single point. Include feeding, development, immunization and family context in the story.~Growth:Change in body size|Development:Acquisition of skills and functional abilities|Gestational age:Duration of a pregnancy|Regression:Loss of previously acquired skills~Growth/Increasing size/Development/Acquiring abilities~Explain why an adult reference range or clinical pattern cannot always be applied to a child.
ophthalmology~Trace light from the front surface to the retina, then the signal to the brain. Distinguish visual acuity from visual field and eye pressure from optic nerve health. Localize the problem before memorizing disease names.~Refraction:Bending of light at an interface|Accommodation:Change in focusing power for near vision|Retina:Light-sensitive neural tissue inside the eye|Optic neuropathy:Damage or dysfunction of the optic nerve~Cataract/Lens opacity/Glaucoma/Optic neuropathy with characteristic damage~Locate the cornea, lens, retina and optic nerve, and describe one job for each.
ent~Follow a route: sound to cochlea, air to lungs or food to esophagus. A structural blockage and a neural problem produce different patterns. Use anatomy to connect hearing, balance, swallowing and voice.~Cochlea:Inner-ear structure that converts sound-related motion to neural signals|Vestibular:Relating to balance and head-motion sensing|Dysphagia:Difficulty swallowing|Stridor:A sound associated with narrowed upper airways~Conductive loss/Reduced sound transmission/Sensorineural loss/Cochlear or neural dysfunction~Trace sound from the external ear to the auditory nerve.
orthopedics~Describe the tissue involved, the mechanical problem and its effect on function. Always think beyond the X-ray to nerves, vessels and soft tissue. Healing needs both a biological environment and appropriate mechanical conditions.~Fracture:A break in bone continuity|Dislocation:Loss of normal joint congruity|Callus:Repair tissue formed during many types of fracture healing|Articular:Relating to a joint surface~Osteoporosis/Reduced bone strength with loss of bone mass or structure/Osteomalacia/Defective bone mineralization~Describe a fracture by location, pattern, displacement and soft-tissue context.
dermatology~Read the skin like a structured description: primary lesion, surface, arrangement, distribution and symptoms. A familiar-looking patch is not automatically a familiar diagnosis. Include nails, hair, mucosa and relevant systemic findings when appropriate.~Macule:A flat color change|Papule:A small raised solid lesion|Vesicle:A small fluid-filled lesion|Scale:Visible shedding or accumulation of outer epidermal material~Morphology/What the lesion looks like/Etiology/What causes the lesion~Describe a skin finding using ordinary words, then translate into morphological terms.
psychiatry~Organize symptoms by time course, mental function, distress and effect on daily life. A symptom is not a moral judgment or a diagnosis by itself. Consider developmental, cultural, medical and substance-related context.~Affect:Observed expression of emotion|Mood:A person's sustained or reported emotional state|Hallucination:A perception-like experience without a matching external stimulus|Cognition:Mental processes such as attention, memory and reasoning~Delirium/Acute fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness/Dementia/Acquired cognitive decline affecting independence~Explain how duration and functional impact change the meaning of a symptom.
radiology~Choose a modality for a question, not because it seems more advanced. Check image quality before interpreting findings, then review systematically. An imaging description becomes clinically meaningful only when connected to the presentation.~Radiolucent:Relatively permits X-rays to pass|Radiopaque:Relatively attenuates X-rays|Contrast:Difference that helps distinguish structures|Cross-sectional:A view through a section of the body~CT/Uses X-rays to create sectional images/MRI/Uses magnetic fields and radiofrequency signals~Match a clinical question to a modality and explain one limitation of that choice.
anesthesia~Think continuously about airway, ventilation, oxygenation, circulation and consciousness. Monitors measure different pieces of physiology. Understand what a drug does and what it does not do; immobility is not evidence of unconsciousness or pain relief.~Analgesia:Reduction or absence of pain|Amnesia:Loss or prevention of memory formation|Ventilation:Movement of air that enables carbon-dioxide elimination|Neuromuscular block:Interruption of transmission to skeletal muscle~Oxygenation/Adding oxygen to blood/Ventilation/Moving air and eliminating carbon dioxide~Explain why a pulse oximeter and a capnograph answer different questions.`;
const SUBJECT_GUIDES={};
GUIDE_RAW.trim().split('\n').forEach(row=>{const[id,overview,terms,comparison,task]=row.split('~');const[c1,d1,c2,d2]=comparison.split('/');SUBJECT_GUIDES[id]={overview,terms:terms.split('|').map(t=>{const i=t.indexOf(':');return[t.slice(0,i),t.slice(i+1)];}),comparison:[[c1,d1],[c2,d2]],task};});

const EXPANSION_RAW=`
foundation-0~The same organ can contain several tissue types. In the stomach, epithelial tissue forms a lining, smooth muscle mixes contents, connective tissue supports the wall and nerves coordinate activity. An organ system is a partnership of organs rather than one giant organ.~Why is the stomach an organ rather than a tissue?~It combines multiple tissue types into a structure with coordinated digestive functions.~A tissue is not simply a smaller version of an organ; the distinction is about organization.
foundation-1~Break a word into pieces, then check its complete definition. Bradycardia combines a slowing prefix with a heart-related root. The name identifies a slow heart rate but does not tell you the cause, significance or treatment. Medical language is a description system, not a substitute for reasoning.~Does the word hepatitis tell you that a virus caused it?~No. It describes liver inflammation, which has infectious and noninfectious causes.~A suffix can identify a process without establishing an etiology.
foundation-2~Directions refer to the person's body in anatomical position, not the observer's view. In a front-facing illustration, the person's right side appears on your left. Pair every directional word with a reference: the elbow is proximal to the wrist, but distal to the shoulder.~Is the elbow proximal or distal to the wrist?~Proximal, because it lies nearer the upper limb's attachment to the trunk.~Do not swap the patient's right with the viewer's right in an anterior-view image.
foundation-3~A concentration difference creates a tendency for particles to spread. A membrane changes which particles can cross and how quickly. Water can move toward a compartment with more effectively nonpenetrating solute; the response also depends on membrane permeability and transport mechanisms.~Why is a cell membrane called selectively permeable?~Some substances cross readily while others need channels, carriers or may be largely excluded.~Diffusion does not require a cell to actively push each molecule downhill.
foundation-4~A negative-feedback loop needs a measured variable and a response that opposes its deviation. If the body becomes warmer, mechanisms that increase heat loss help limit that rise. The word negative describes the direction of feedback, not whether the outcome is harmful.~If a response makes the original disturbance smaller, what type of feedback is present?~Negative feedback, because the response opposes rather than reinforces the change.~Positive does not mean good, and negative does not mean bad, in feedback terminology.
foundation-5~Track one red cell: systemic veins, right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary arteries, lung capillaries, pulmonary veins, left atrium, left ventricle and aorta. Valves support one-way flow. Oxygen content changes along the route, but vessel names follow direction relative to the heart.~Why are pulmonary arteries still called arteries?~They carry blood away from the heart, even though their blood has relatively low oxygen content.~Blue in a textbook usually indicates relative oxygen content; human venous blood is not blue.
foundation-6~Air in an alveolus and blood in a neighboring capillary are separated by a thin barrier. Oxygen must cross that barrier, bind or dissolve in blood and then reach tissues. Air moving into a lung is therefore only one step in oxygen delivery.~Can a lung receive air but exchange gas poorly?~Yes. Impaired blood flow or problems with the exchange barrier can limit effective gas exchange.~Ventilation, gas exchange and tissue oxygen delivery are related but not interchangeable.
foundation-7~Mechanical digestion reduces food particle size, while chemical digestion breaks molecular bonds with enzymes. Absorption moves the resulting substances across the intestinal lining. A person could have a problem with either stage, so digestion and absorption should be learned separately.~What is the difference between digestion and absorption?~Digestion breaks food into smaller molecules; absorption transfers substances across the gut lining into the body.~Swallowing food does not mean its nutrients have already entered the bloodstream.
foundation-8~Imagine filtering a mixed basket, then reclaiming useful items. Kidney filtration is followed by selective tubular handling: many filtered substances return to blood, while others are added to tubular fluid. Final excretion reflects all three processes, not filtration alone.~A substance is filtered and completely reabsorbed, with no secretion. Is it excreted?~No. In this simplified example, filtration minus reabsorption leaves no net urinary excretion.~A large filtered volume does not imply an equally large daily urine volume.
foundation-9~A circulating hormone reaches many tissues, but the response depends on receptors and intracellular machinery. The same signal can have different effects in different tissues. Neural and hormonal communication also interact; they are not two isolated control systems.~Why can one hormone affect two organs differently?~The organs may express different receptors or connect receptor activation to different cellular responses.~A hormone does not have the same effect on every cell it reaches.
foundation-10~Immune defense includes recognition, response and regulation. A first encounter can generate adaptive cells that respond more efficiently later. Protection varies with the organism and type of response; immune memory does not mean an infection is impossible forever.~Why can a later immune response differ from the first one?~Memory lymphocytes can recognize the antigen and contribute to a faster or more effective response.~Inflammation and infection are not synonyms; inflammation also occurs without microbes.
foundation-11~A complaint guides the assessment, while examination and tests add evidence. For example, a patient may report feeling breathless, while respiratory rate is measured separately. A normal measurement does not automatically invalidate the experience; the pieces need to be interpreted together.~Classify reported chest discomfort and a measured pulse as symptom or sign.~Chest discomfort is a symptom; the measured pulse is a sign.~A test result is evidence within a clinical context, not an independent diagnosis.
foundation-12~Suppose a risk decreases from 2 in 100 to 1 in 100. The relative reduction is 50%, but the absolute reduction is 1 percentage point. Both statements can be correct, yet they communicate different things. Start by asking what the baseline risk was.~What is the absolute reduction from 2% to 1%?~One percentage point, or one fewer event per 100 people in this example.~A large relative change can accompany a small absolute change.
foundation-13~Reading a familiar sentence can feel like mastery even when the answer cannot be produced unaided. Retrieval practice tests whether the information is accessible. After attempting recall, compare with the explanation, correct the error and revisit later rather than repeatedly rereading without testing.~Why attempt an answer before turning a flashcard?~It exposes what can actually be retrieved and identifies gaps that recognition alone may hide.~Marking a card familiar is not the same as being able to explain it.
anatomy-0~Connect shape to work. Thin epithelial layers support exchange, while thicker or layered arrangements provide protection. Tissue classification also depends on organization and cell features. When viewing a histology image, first locate a surface or lumen, then examine the layers and surrounding support.~Why might a thin lining be useful at an exchange surface?~It can reduce the distance substances must cross; the full exchange process also depends on gradients and permeability.~Do not identify a tissue from color alone; stains and image preparation change appearance.
anatomy-1~A peripheral nerve carries fibers from several spinal roots, and one root contributes to multiple peripheral nerves. That is why a root lesion and a terminal nerve lesion need not produce the same pattern. Combine motor actions, sensory territory and anatomical course when localizing weakness.~Why is a C7 root problem not identical to a radial nerve injury?~C7 fibers are distributed through more than one peripheral nerve, while the radial nerve contains fibers from several roots.~A memorable sign such as wrist drop suggests a pattern but does not identify every lesion level.
anatomy-3~Follow the two cardiac circuits without skipping chambers. The atria receive blood and the ventricles eject it. Pulmonary and systemic circulation are connected in series, so a sustained mismatch in average output cannot continue without blood accumulating somewhere. Wall thickness reflects workload, not simply the amount of blood contained.~Why is the left ventricular wall normally thicker than the right?~It generates the pressure needed for the higher-resistance systemic circulation.~The left ventricle does not normally pump a larger average volume than the right in steady state.
anatomy-4~Localization begins with the side, level and type of deficit. A long motor pathway can be affected above or below its crossing, changing the relationship between lesion side and weakness. The same principle applies to sensory pathways, but their crossing sites and routes differ.~Why does the site of a pathway's crossing matter?~It helps predict whether a lesion produces effects on the same or opposite side of the body.~Do not assume every sensory and motor pathway crosses at the same level.
physiology-0~Membrane potential is a voltage difference, not a large separation of all the cell's charged particles. Selective permeability and ion gradients determine it. The sodium–potassium pump helps maintain the gradients over time, while ion channels determine many rapid changes in electrical behavior.~Does the sodium–potassium pump alone explain the rapid upstroke of a neuronal action potential?~No. Rapid channel-mediated ion movement produces the upstroke; the pump supports the gradients on which signaling depends.~Resting potential, equilibrium potential and action potential are different concepts.
physiology-2~Keep flow and pressure separate. Cardiac output describes blood volume pumped per minute; pressure depends on both flow and vascular resistance. A faster heart rate does not guarantee higher output because filling and stroke volume may change. Use the equation to organize reasoning, then check its physiological assumptions.~At 70 beats per minute and 70 mL per beat, what is cardiac output?~70 × 70 = 4,900 mL/min, or 4.9 L/min. The units show why rate and volume must both be included.~Stroke volume is volume per beat, whereas cardiac output is volume per minute.
physiology-3~The ventilation–perfusion ratio compares air reaching alveoli with blood reaching their capillaries. Very low perfusion wastes ventilation; very low ventilation leaves blood poorly exposed to fresh gas. Real lungs contain regions with different ratios, so an average value can hide regional mismatch.~An alveolus receives air but essentially no blood. Which side of the pair is missing?~Perfusion is missing, so the ventilated region contributes to physiological dead-space behavior.~A well-ventilated region is not necessarily a well-functioning gas-exchange unit.
physiology-4~Use a mass-balance approach for each substance. The quantity appearing in urine depends on filtration, reabsorption and secretion. Different solutes follow different patterns, and the kidney changes their handling according to physiological needs. ADH mainly affects water permeability, not an identical increase in all solute transport.~A substance has 100 units filtered, 80 reabsorbed and 10 secreted. How many are excreted?~100 − 80 + 10 = 30 units. All quantities must refer to the same time interval.~Reabsorption moves material toward blood; secretion moves it toward tubular fluid.
biochemistry-0~An enzyme lowers the activation-energy barrier and changes reaction speed; it does not change the reaction's equilibrium position. In the ideal competitive model, inhibitor and substrate compete for the active site. More substrate can overcome this competition, explaining the preserved maximal velocity.~If Vmax is unchanged but apparent Km increases in an ideal model, which inhibition pattern fits?~Competitive inhibition. More substrate is needed to reach half the same maximum velocity.~Km is not a universal direct measure of binding affinity in every enzyme mechanism.
biochemistry-1~Place pathways in compartments before linking them. Glycolysis is cytosolic; pyruvate can be reduced to lactate or enter mitochondrial metabolism in cells with mitochondria. Mature red cells lack mitochondria, so their ATP generation cannot depend on oxidative phosphorylation.~Why do mature red cells rely on glycolysis for ATP?~They lack mitochondria and therefore cannot use mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation.~The presence of oxygen alone does not give a cell mitochondria or the capacity to use every pathway.
pathology-0~Hypertrophy and hyperplasia describe different routes to enlargement. Existing cells can become larger, cell number can increase, or both processes can contribute. Adaptation has limits: prolonged stress can eventually produce injury. A change that starts as compensation is not automatically harmless forever.~A tissue enlarges because each cell is bigger, without an increase in cell number. Name the process.~Hypertrophy. Hyperplasia would instead involve an increased number of cells.~Metaplasia is a change in differentiated cell type, not simply an increase in size or number.
pathology-3~Read grade and stage as answers to different questions. Grade concerns microscopic appearance and related biological features, while stage describes extent. A tumor can be small yet poorly differentiated, or larger yet relatively well differentiated. Exact grading and staging systems depend on tumor type.~Can a low-stage tumor still have a high grade?~Yes. Limited extent and abnormal microscopic features can coexist because stage and grade describe different dimensions.~Do not use grade as a synonym for spread.
pathology-4~Start anemia reasoning with production, loss and destruction. Cell size helps narrow possibilities, while a reticulocyte response gives information about marrow output. A small red-cell pattern does not by itself prove iron deficiency; inherited hemoglobin disorders and other causes can also produce microcytosis.~Why is a low hemoglobin result only the beginning of the diagnosis?~It establishes an anemia pattern but does not explain whether the cause is reduced production, blood loss or increased destruction.~Do not prescribe iron in a study vignette solely because the word anemia appears.
pharmacology-0~A concentration–effect curve distinguishes how much drug is required from how large the effect can become. Drug A might produce an effect at a smaller concentration than drug B but have a lower maximum. Pharmacokinetics adds another layer: identical doses may not produce identical concentrations.~If drug A reaches a given effect at a lower concentration but has a lower maximum, which property is greater?~Its potency for that specified effect is greater, but its maximal efficacy in that setting is lower.~More potent does not automatically mean more effective, safer or clinically preferable.
pharmacology-1~Receptor names help organize effects, but an organ can express several receptor subtypes. A drug's apparent selectivity depends on exposure, and patient physiology affects the result. Separate the direct receptor effect from compensatory reflexes that may change the observed whole-body response.~Why might a receptor-selective drug produce additional effects at higher exposure?~Selectivity is relative; increasing concentration can engage other targets and change the balance of effects.~One receptor label does not predict every response in every tissue.
pharmacology-4~An antimicrobial target must be present and accessible. Bacterial cell-wall drugs do not have the same target in viruses. Even when a target exists, penetration, resistance and host factors can limit activity. This explains why selecting treatment involves more than memorizing an organism–drug pair.~Why is a bacterial cell-wall inhibitor not a routine treatment for a viral infection?~Viruses lack the bacterial cell-wall synthesis machinery targeted by that class.~A broad-spectrum antibiotic is not universally better than a narrower, appropriate agent.
microbiology-0~Specimen quality matters before any test is run. An inappropriate sample, contamination or collection after treatment can change what is detected. A positive result must be matched to the suspected site and clinical pattern; detection of an organism is not always evidence of invasive disease.~Why can a highly accurate test still be unhelpful if the wrong specimen is collected?~It may accurately describe material that does not represent the site or process being investigated.~A laboratory result cannot repair an unsuitable clinical question or specimen.
microbiology-5~Cleaning removes material that can interfere with later processing. Disinfection and sterilization describe different endpoints and are selected according to how an item will be used. Time, concentration, temperature and equipment compatibility matter; naming a chemical alone does not establish the achieved level of processing.~Why is cleaning needed before many disinfection or sterilization processes?~Residual organic material can shield microorganisms and interfere with the process.~A visibly clean instrument is not automatically sterile.
forensic-0~Capacity is decision-specific and must be distinguished from simple agreement. A person needs enough understanding to consider relevant information and choose voluntarily. Clear documentation records the discussion and decision, not just a signature. Legal requirements vary by context and must be checked against current guidance.~Does a signed form alone demonstrate fully informed consent?~No. The process must include capacity, relevant information, understanding and voluntariness.~Consent is a communication process, not merely a document.
forensic-1~Evidence can lose value if its origin or handling becomes uncertain. A chain-of-custody record links collection, labeling, sealing, transfer and receipt. Keep descriptive observations separate from conclusions so that another examiner can understand what was actually seen and how the opinion was reached.~Why document each transfer of a specimen?~It helps establish identity, continuity and accountability for the material being analyzed.~A plausible conclusion does not compensate for undocumented evidence handling.
community-0~Incidence asks about new events, while prevalence describes the existing burden. A condition can have high prevalence because people live with it for a long time, even when relatively few new cases arise each year. The denominator and time frame must match the question.~Why might prevalence rise when survival improves, even if incidence is unchanged?~People remain in the pool of existing cases for longer, increasing the number living with the condition.~Incidence and prevalence cannot be substituted without considering disease duration and population dynamics.
community-2~Construct the two-by-two table before using a formula. Among people who truly have disease, sensitivity counts the positive tests. Among those without disease, specificity counts the negative tests. Predictive values reverse the conditioning: they begin with a test result and ask about disease status.~If 100 people have a disease and 90 test positive, what is sensitivity?~90/100 = 90%. This says nothing by itself about specificity, which needs data from people without disease.~A 90% sensitivity does not mean every positive result has a 90% chance of representing disease.
medicine-0~Shock is a failure of adequate tissue perfusion, and blood pressure is only one part of its assessment. A pump problem, low circulating volume, inappropriate vasodilation or an obstruction can each reduce effective flow. Organ findings and the time course help distinguish mechanisms.~Why is shock not simply defined as a low blood-pressure reading?~Tissue perfusion can be inadequate before marked hypotension, and a pressure value alone does not describe organ blood flow.~Hypotension and shock overlap but are not identical concepts.
medicine-1~Heart failure is a syndrome rather than one isolated measurement. Pumping and filling problems can both impair function. Ejection fraction is the fraction of ventricular end-diastolic volume ejected per beat; it is not a direct measurement of every aspect of cardiac performance.~Why can heart failure occur with a preserved ejection fraction?~Abnormal filling and other functional problems can produce the syndrome even when the ejected fraction remains preserved.~A normal-looking fraction does not prove normal filling pressures or exercise capacity.
medicine-4~Think of insulin resistance as reduced tissue responsiveness and beta-cell dysfunction as inadequate compensation. These can coexist in type 2 diabetes. Contrast that with the usual autoimmune loss of beta cells in type 1 diabetes, while remembering that real clinical classification may need more than age or body size.~Does adult age alone establish type 2 diabetes?~No. Different diabetes types can present in adults, so the overall clinical and laboratory context matters.~Age and appearance are clues, not definitive biological classifications.
surgery-2~A hollow tube can stop moving contents because of a physical blockage or a motility problem. Pressure and distension may increase upstream. If blood supply becomes compromised, the problem shifts from impaired passage toward threatened tissue viability. This is why obstruction and strangulation must be distinguished.~What additional process makes strangulation more serious than uncomplicated obstruction?~Reduced blood supply introduces ischemic injury and a risk of tissue death.~The presence of bowel sounds alone cannot safely classify every abdominal emergency.
surgery-3~A hernia has a defect, a sac or protrusion and contents. Reducibility describes whether the protrusion can return, while strangulation describes threatened perfusion. The two terms address different questions; a nonreducible hernia requires assessment, but nonreducibility does not by itself prove necrosis.~What is the difference between incarceration and strangulation?~Incarceration means irreducibility; strangulation means the blood supply is compromised.~Do not treat irreducible and ischemic as interchangeable descriptions.
obgyn-0~Keep the ovarian and uterine cycles aligned. Follicles develop while the endometrium proliferates; after ovulation, the corpus luteum produces progesterone, supporting secretory change. Hormonal feedback is not uniformly negative throughout the cycle, and the timing of ovulation varies with cycle characteristics.~Which structure produces much of the progesterone in the normal luteal phase?~The corpus luteum, formed from the ovulated follicle.~Day 14 is a teaching example for a typical 28-day cycle, not a universal ovulation date.
obgyn-3~The four-T framework organizes postpartum hemorrhage mechanisms: poor uterine contraction, injury, retained tissue and clotting problems. It is a memory aid for assessment, not a treatment algorithm. More than one mechanism can be present, so finding one possibility does not exclude others.~Which T includes inadequate uterine contraction?~Tone, referring to the uterus's ability to contract and compress vessels after delivery.~A mnemonic helps organize causes but does not replace emergency assessment or current protocols.
pediatrics-0~Growth charts compare a child's measurements with an appropriate reference, but interpretation requires repeated measurements and context. A measurement can be wrong because of technique or recording error. Development adds a different dimension: skills can progress normally even when size is at a lower percentile.~Why repeat and plot measurements instead of interpreting one point alone?~A trajectory helps distinguish consistent growth from an emerging change and can reveal measurement errors.~A low percentile alone is not a complete diagnosis of disease or malnutrition.
pediatrics-2~Birth changes the source of gas exchange from placenta to lungs and requires coordinated circulatory adaptation. Gestational maturity influences the ability to breathe, feed and maintain temperature. Newborn findings must be interpreted by age and gestation rather than with older-child assumptions.~Why does gestational age matter when assessing a newborn?~Prematurity changes organ maturity, expected adaptation and the risks associated with particular findings.~Newborns are not simply small older children; timing and maturity affect interpretation.
ophthalmology-0~An optical system must focus light onto the retinal plane. In myopia, distant parallel rays focus too early in an uncorrected eye; a diverging lens can adjust the optical path. Visual acuity testing quantifies resolution, but it does not measure every part of visual function.~What kind of lens classically corrects simple myopia?~A diverging, or concave, lens moves the effective focus back toward the retinal plane.~Normal central acuity does not exclude a visual-field defect.
ophthalmology-3~The optic nerve is the structure damaged in glaucoma. Intraocular pressure is an important risk factor, but the diagnosis concerns characteristic damage and functional change. Cataract affects a different structure, the lens, so the two can impair vision through very different mechanisms.~Why is an eye-pressure measurement alone insufficient to exclude glaucoma?~Some people develop glaucomatous damage without an elevated pressure at the time of measurement.~Lens opacity and optic nerve damage should not be grouped as the same cause of visual loss.
ent-0~Sound waves move the tympanic membrane and middle-ear ossicles. These mechanical movements ultimately create fluid motion in the cochlea, where sensory hair cells help convert movement into neural activity. A problem before this conversion differs from a problem in sensory cells or neural pathways.~Why can middle-ear disease cause hearing loss even if the auditory nerve is intact?~It can reduce the mechanical transmission of sound to the cochlea, producing a conductive component.~Hearing depends on a chain of structures, not only the auditory nerve.
ent-2~Balance combines vestibular information with vision and body-position signals. A mismatch between these inputs can create a sensation of movement or instability. Distinguish the experience of spinning from faintness or general imbalance, then consider associated features rather than assuming every dizzy episode is an ear disorder.~Is dizziness always caused by the inner ear?~No. The term covers different experiences with vestibular, neurological, cardiovascular and other possible mechanisms.~Vertigo describes a symptom; it does not name a single disease.
orthopedics-0~A fracture description should communicate more than its existence. Specify bone and segment, pattern, displacement, joint involvement and whether there is communication with the outside. Surrounding soft tissue, blood supply and nerves affect urgency and healing independently of the radiographic appearance.~Can a small skin wound be important when evaluating a fracture?~Yes. It may communicate with the fracture, making the injury open even when the visible wound is small.~The size of the external wound does not reliably describe all underlying damage.
orthopedics-3~Bone strength depends on amount, architecture and material properties. Osteoporosis involves reduced strength and greater fragility, often without symptoms until fracture. Defective mineralization is a different concept and helps distinguish osteomalacia from low bone mass alone.~Why is osteoporosis sometimes called silent?~Bone weakening may not produce clear symptoms before a fragility fracture occurs.~Bone pain is not required for osteoporosis, and low density does not describe every possible bone disorder.
dermatology-0~Translate the first visual impression into a structured description. Note whether a lesion is flat or raised, solid or fluid-filled, then describe color, surface and distribution. Several diseases can share one morphology, so the description is a starting point for a differential.~A small, flat patch differs in color but cannot be felt above the surface. Which primary lesion term fits?~Macule. The term describes morphology rather than the cause of the color change.~A lesion name is not automatically a disease name.
dermatology-1~The skin barrier limits water loss and exposure to environmental irritants. Barrier dysfunction and inflammation can reinforce one another in eczema. Scratching may temporarily relieve itch but can further injure the barrier. Psoriasis has a different immune-mediated pattern and should not be inferred from scaling alone.~Why can repeated scratching worsen an itchy inflammatory skin condition?~It can damage the barrier and sustain irritation and inflammation.~Any scaly patch is not automatically psoriasis; distribution and the full clinical pattern matter.
psychiatry-0~The mental status examination is a snapshot, while the history describes change over time. Reported mood and observed affect may differ. A formulation combines biological, psychological and social information rather than reducing a person to one label or a single unusual experience.~Why is the history needed in addition to a mental status examination?~It shows duration, course, context and functional changes that may not be visible in a single interview.~A normal-looking moment does not exclude a disorder with episodic or fluctuating symptoms.
psychiatry-1~Mood-disorder assessment examines a cluster of changes in mood, energy, sleep, thinking and behavior over time. Mania is not simply being cheerful or productive. The degree of impairment, duration, associated symptoms and possible medical or substance explanations all matter.~Why does ordinary happiness not by itself indicate mania?~Mania involves a defined syndrome with abnormal mood and increased activity or energy, associated features and substantial clinical impact.~Do not diagnose a mood disorder from one adjective or one isolated day.
radiology-0~Match the physical method to the information needed. X-rays show differences in attenuation; ultrasound uses reflected sound; MRI creates contrast through magnetic and radiofrequency interactions. More detail in one tissue does not mean superiority for every clinical question or every patient.~Which of CT, ultrasound and MRI uses ionizing X-rays?~CT. Ultrasound and MRI use different physical mechanisms and do not use ionizing X-rays.~No ionizing radiation does not mean a modality has no limitations, contraindications or practical risks.
radiology-1~Technical factors can imitate or obscure disease. Rotation changes apparent alignment, inspiration affects lung appearance and projection affects apparent heart size. Review image quality first, then use a consistent search pattern. A single bright or dark area is a finding to explain, not a final diagnosis.~Why assess projection before interpreting apparent cardiac enlargement?~Projection can magnify structures and change the apparent size of the cardiac silhouette.~Do not compare unlike imaging projections as if all technical conditions were identical.
anesthesia-1~Ventilation affects carbon-dioxide removal, while oxygenation concerns oxygen entering blood. Supplemental oxygen may maintain saturation for a time even when ventilation is inadequate. Different monitors therefore answer different physiological questions, and their values must be interpreted together.~Why can oxygen saturation appear acceptable despite impaired ventilation?~Oxygen administration and physiological reserves may temporarily preserve saturation while carbon dioxide rises.~A pulse oximeter cannot replace assessment of ventilation.
anesthesia-2~Separate unconsciousness, analgesia, amnesia and muscle relaxation. Different drugs contribute different effects, and movement is not a reliable measure of awareness when neuromuscular transmission is blocked. This is a mechanistic learning point, not a guide to administering anesthesia.~A drug prevents skeletal muscle movement. Does that establish that the person is unconscious?~No. Neuromuscular blockade does not itself provide unconsciousness, amnesia or analgesia.~Immobility and unconsciousness are not equivalent.`;
